	uchar sk[MLKEM1024_secretbytes]);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions implement module-latice-based key-encapsulation as described in the FIPS 203 standard.
The standard defines three security levels that are represented here by the 512, 768, and 1024 function variants.
None of these functions return allocated memory, yet must allocate memory for temporaries due to their size.
As such, allocation failures are still possible. The callers of each function are required to provide correctly
sized buffers for input and output. The array sized constants used above are defined within
.BR <libsec.h> .
.PP
.IR Mlkem512_keypair ,
.IR mlkem768_keypair ,
and
.I mlkem1024_keypair
generate a new key pair and write the public key into
.B pk
and the secret key into
.BR sk .
These functions return 0 on success and non-zero on allocation failure.
.PP
.IR Mlkem512_enc ,
.IR mlkem768_enc ,
and
.I mlkem1024_enc
encapsulate a shared secret provided in
.B ss
into
.B ct
using the provided public key
.BR pk .
These functions return 0 on success and non-zero if the provided public key is malformed or if an allocation failure occurred.
.PP
.IR Mlkem512_dec ,
.IR mlkem768_dec ,
and
.I mlkem1024_dec
decapsulate the cipher text provided in
.B ct
into
.B ss
using the provided secret key
.BR sk .
Note, decapsulation itself has no checks for ensuring that the provided cipher text was properly encapsulated with the matching public key.
These functions therefore must first perform decapsulation, then encapsulation and check if the resulting cipher text is the same.
However even if resulting cipher texts do not match these functions do not return an error, instead they return a rejection key in accordance with the FIPS standard.
Thus, the resulting shared secret should be used only in ways that require the other party to also assert their knowledge of the shared secret.
Commonly this is done by using the shared secret as input to a stream cipher.
These functions return 0 on success and non-zero if the provided secret key is malformed or if an allocation failure occurred.
